# 🧠 BloodHound Analysis – Active Directory Attack Path Mapping

## 📌 Overview
BloodHound is used to map relationships in Active Directory to identify:
- Privilege escalation paths
- Misconfigured permissions
- Attack paths to Domain Admin

---

## 🎯 Objective
Identify the **shortest path to high privilege accounts**

---

## 🧭 Workflow (High-Level)

1. Collect directory data (authorized lab/tooling)
2. Import into BloodHound
3. Analyze relationships
4. Identify attack paths

---

## 🔍 What to Look For

### 🔑 High Value Targets
- Domain Admins
- Enterprise Admins
- Service Accounts

---

### 🔗 Dangerous Relationships
- “User → AdminTo → Computer”
- “User → MemberOf → Privileged Group”
- “GenericAll / GenericWrite permissions”

---

### ⚠️ Common Weaknesses
- Over-permissioned accounts
- Stale admin privileges
- Nested group abuse

---

## 📊 Key Queries (Conceptual)

- Shortest path to Domain Admin
- Users with admin rights on multiple systems
- Kerberoastable accounts

---

## 🛡️ Defensive Insight

- Monitor excessive LDAP queries
- Detect large-scale enumeration
- Audit privileged group membership

---

## 🧠 DPCI Notes

- Export graphs as evidence
- Document relationships clearly
- Use visuals in reports
